Presidential elections were held in [[Croatia]] on 27 December 2009 and 10 January 2010. Twelve candidates participated in the first round, prior to a run-off between first-round winner [[Ivo Josipović]] and runner-up [[Milan Bandić]]. In the run-off, Josipović won a landslide victory, receiving 60.3% of the vote becoming the first elected president nominated by the [[Social Democratic Party of Croatia]] (SDP).<ref>{{citation | title = Social Democrat Ivo Josipovic elected Croatia president | url = https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/8450380.stm | publisher = BBC News | date = 11 January 2010 | access-date = 10 January 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20100111051904/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/8450380.stm | archive-date = 11 January 2010 | url-status = live | df = dmy-all }}</ref> The incumbent president [[Stjepan Mesić]], who was first elected in [[2000 Croatian presidential election|2000]] as the candidate of the [[Croatian People's Party-Liberal Democrats|Croatian People's Party]] and re-elected in [[2005 Croatian presidential election|2005]] as an [[Independent politician|independent]], was ineligible to seek re-election to a third term due to term limits.

As the incumbent was ineligible for re-election, several candidates took the opportunity to run for the presidency. Most mainstream Croatian political parties participated in the elections either by nominating a candidate or endorsing one. The relatively low nomination threshold (ten thousand signatures in a country of four million voters), turmoil in the largest political party ([[Croatian Democratic Union]], HDZ) due to the departure of long-time leader [[Ivo Sanader]] and the ongoing economic crisis, as well as a significant one-man revolt in the second-largest party ([[Social Democratic Party of Croatia]], SDP), led to a record number of candidates contesting the elections.

In the first round, the left of centre vote was split between 3–4 candidates, while the right of centre vote was split between 5–6 candidates. Owing to the increased fragmentation of the right, two candidates who were both long-time members of SDP progressed to the run-off. The election polls accurately predicted the winner, while the runner-up projections were usually within a statistical margin of error, lifting many candidates' hopes and enabling a sense of drama stirred by the Croatian media.

The second round was marked by more intense polarization. The Bandić campaign shifted their political message significantly to the right, with the Josipović campaign put on the defensive. However, a sudden reappearance of Sanader interrupted the election campaign and cemented Josipović's advantage; in the end, he won by a large margin, and was inaugurated as the third President of the Republic of Croatia on 18 February.

== Background and rules ==
[[File:Stjepan Mesić (2004-02-25) (cropped).jpg|thumb|[[Stjepan Mesić]], the term-limited incumbent president whose term expired on 18 February 2010.]]
After serving two consecutive five-year terms, the incumbent [[President of Croatia|president]] [[Stjepan Mesić]] was not eligible to run in the 2009 election.

The elections officially began on 4 November, with the start of the period for the collecting of signatures needed to become an official candidate.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://metro-portal.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/poceli-teci-rokovi-za-predsjednicke-izbore | title=Počeli teći rokovi za predsjedničke izbore | date=November 5, 2009 | work=metro-portal.hr | language=hr | access-date=2009-11-28}}</ref> Each candidate had 12 days to collect 10,000 written signatures from citizens who are eligible to vote. After the 12 days expired, the signatures were verified and on November 18 the State Electoral Commission announced the 12 candidates that managed to collect the necessary number of signatures. The next day the official 37-day campaign began and went until 24 hours before Election Day, which happened to be [[Christmas Day]], midnight.

The first round was held on 27 December<ref name="vecernji-init">{{cite web | work=[[Večernji list]] | date=October 30, 2009 | url=http://www.vecernji.hr/vijesti/predsjednicki-izbori-sazvani-27-prosinca-drugi-krug-10-sijecnja-clanak-42821 | title=Prvi krug predsjedničkih izbora u Hrvatskoj održat će se 27. prosinca | language=hr | access-date=2009-11-28 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091102064025/http://www.vecernji.hr/vijesti/predsjednicki-izbori-sazvani-27-prosinca-drugi-krug-10-sijecnja-clanak-42821 | archive-date=November 2, 2009 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> and no candidate managed to obtain [[Two-round system|50% + one vote]], so a second round was held on 10 January 2010<ref name="vecernji-init"/> between the two candidates that achieved the most votes, [[Ivo Josipović]] and [[Milan Bandić]]. Each candidate could have withdrawn from the election at any time.

== Campaign before the official start ==
Although officially the campaign started on 19 November, in reality it began as early as summer of 2009. The main political parties had not yet announced their nominees for president in the summer, but certain high-ranking politicians{{who|date=November 2012}} had expressed their interest in running very early.

The governing [[Croatian Democratic Union|HDZ]] saw considerable drama in selecting their candidate for president. Ever since late 2008, there was much speculation that [[Prime Minister of Croatia|prime minister]] [[Ivo Sanader]] would run for the presidency, although he frequently denied such speculation.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nacional.hr/clanak/52789/bitka-za-predsjednika-u-trolistu-sanader-cacic-jurcic |title=Bitka za predsjednika u trolistu Sanader-Čačić-Jurčić? |trans-title=Battle for the presidential office in Sanader-Čačić-Jurčić triangle? |date=3 February 2009 |publisher=[[Nacional (weekly)|Nacional]] |language=hr |access-date=14 December 2009 |archive-date=13 June 2011 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110613171905/http://www.nacional.hr/clanak/52789/bitka-za-predsjednika-u-trolistu-sanader-cacic-jurcic |author=Srećko Jurdana }}</ref> After the [[2009 Croatian local elections|local elections]], the speculation grew as no other candidates publicly hinted they would run. However, Ivo Sanader unexpectedly decided not only to not run for the presidency, but to resign as prime minister and retire from active politics altogether, announcing his decision on 1 July 2009.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://in.reuters.com/article/worldNews/idINIndia-40733720090701 | title=Croatia's PM Sanader resigns, quits politics | date=1 July 2009 | work=[[Reuters]] | access-date=14 December 2009 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090705164725/http://in.reuters.com/article/worldNews/idINIndia-40733720090701 | archive-date=5 July 2009 | url-status=dead | df=dmy-all }}</ref>

Sanader nominated [[Andrija Hebrang (son)|Andrija Hebrang]] as the party's candidate for president, a decision that was backed by the remainder of the party's presidency after a while,<ref>{{cite web | work=Metro-portal.hr | date=July 29, 2009 | url=http://metro-portal.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/andrija-hebrang-predsjednicki-kandidat-hdz-a | title=Andrija Hebrang predsjednički kandidat HDZ-a | language=hr | access-date=2009-11-28 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090802022236/http://metro-portal.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/andrija-hebrang-predsjednicki-kandidat-hdz-a | archive-date=August 2, 2009 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> but not before widespread rumours started about the HDZ member [[Nadan Vidošević]], a prominent businessman, being the presidency's favorite candidate. Vidošević however rejected these claims.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.index.hr/vijesti/clanak/jutarnje-prognoze-nadan-vidosevic-kandidat-hdza-za-predsjednika/443182.aspx | title = Vidošević za Index: U predsjedničku utrku idem jedino kao nezavisni kandidat! | date = July 24, 2009 | work = [[Index.hr]]/[[Jutarnji list]] | language = hr | access-date = December 25, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20090727175143/http://www.index.hr/vijesti/clanak/jutarnje-prognoze-nadan-vidosevic-kandidat-hdza-za-predsjednika/443182.aspx | archive-date = July 27, 2009 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> Hebrang also hesitated before accepting the party nomination, explaining it with health reasons.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://web1.slobodnadalmacija.hr/Hrvatska/tabid/66/articleType/ArticleView/articleId/63730/Default.aspx | work = [[Slobodna Dalmacija]] | title = Andrija Hebrang zdrav i spreman za predsjedničku utrku | language = hr | date = July 27, 2009 | access-date = December 26, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110721102027/http://web1.slobodnadalmacija.hr/Hrvatska/tabid/66/articleType/ArticleView/articleId/63730/Default.aspx | archive-date = July 21, 2011 | url-status = dead | df = mdy-all }}</ref> Vidošević formally announced his bid as an independent candidate on September 2, and was expelled from his party soon after that.<ref name="vidosevic-hdz">{{cite web | url = http://www.r-1.hr/nadan_vido%C5%A1evic_izbacen_iz_hdz_a/29819_4 | title = Nadan Vidošević izbačen iz HDZ-a | publisher = R1 regionalna radio mreža | work = R1 Portal r-1.hr | language = hr | date = September 2, 2009 | access-date = September 8, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110721101931/http://www.r-1.hr/nadan_vido%C5%A1evic_izbacen_iz_hdz_a/29819_4 | archive-date = July 21, 2011 | url-status = dead | df = mdy-all }}</ref> Also, Ivo Sanader was not the only member of the [[Croatian Government|cabinet]] to resign as Minister of Education [[Dragan Primorac]] also announced his retirement on the very same day as the prime minister. There was much speculation that Primorac resigned as he was not his party's candidate for president and that he resigned so he could run as an independent. The speculation proved to be true and Primorac announced his candidacy on 9 November 2009.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.index.hr/vijesti/clanak/index-doznaje-dragan-primorac-krenuo-u-predsjednicku-utrku-za-pantovcak/449844.aspx | title = Index doznaje: Dragan Primorac krenuo u predsjedničku utrku za Pantovčak | work = [[Index.hr]] | date = September 11, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = September 11, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20090914040443/http://www.index.hr/vijesti/clanak/index-doznaje-dragan-primorac-krenuo-u-predsjednicku-utrku-za-pantovcak/449844.aspx | archive-date = September 14, 2009 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> He was then also ejected from HDZ membership.<ref>{{cite web|publisher=[[Business.hr]]|url=http://www.business.hr/vijesti/video-kosorica-izbacila-primorca-iz-hdz-a|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130217154620/http://www.business.hr/vijesti/video-kosorica-izbacila-primorca-iz-hdz-a|url-status=dead|archive-date=17 February 2013|language=hr|title=Kosorica izbacila Primorca iz HDZ-a|trans-title=Kosor ejects Primorac from HDZ|date=5 November 2009|access-date=7 November 2012}}</ref>

The [[Social Democratic Party of Croatia]] also saw some controversy in the process of nominating a candidate. Immediately after the [[2009 Zagreb local elections|2009 local elections]] and [[Milan Bandić]]'s landslide victory in [[Zagreb]] as a candidate of the Social Democrats, there was much talk of his potential candidacy for president. Before the election, many influential members of SDP urged [[Zoran Milanović]], the president of the party, to intervene and nominate someone other than Bandić, referring to numerous corruption and malversation allegations made against him during his tenure as the [[mayor of Zagreb]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nacional.hr/en/clanak/44700/bandic-and-milanovic-fighting-it-out-for-zagreb |title=Bandic and Milanovic fighting it out for Zagreb |date=21 April 2008 |work=[[Nacional (weekly)|Nacional]] |access-date=14 December 2009 |author=Robert Bajruši |archive-date=13 June 2011 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110613171842/http://www.nacional.hr/en/clanak/44700/bandic-and-milanovic-fighting-it-out-for-zagreb }}</ref> However, Milanović denied the widespread rumours about internal turmoil and publicly supported Milan Bandić in the mayoral campaign,<ref>{{cite web | url=http://colonia.tino.javno.info/gn.php?m=3&rbr=20856&kategorija=0 | title=Milanović: Došao sam kao podrška | date=21 April 2009 | work=[[Javno.hr]] | language=hr | access-date=14 December 2009 }}{{Dead link|date=April 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> making him the target of an open letter signed by many prominent left-wing intellectuals, in which they attacked Bandić and endorsed [[Josip Kregar]].<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.h-alter.org/vijesti/vijesti/otvoreno-pismo-milanovic-izdao-socijaldemokraciju | title=Otvoreno pismo: Milanović izdao socijaldemokraciju | date=9 July 2008 | work=[[H-alter.org]] | language=hr | access-date=14 December 2009 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110727020859/http://www.h-alter.org/vijesti/vijesti/otvoreno-pismo-milanovic-izdao-socijaldemokraciju | archive-date=27 July 2011 | url-status=live | df=dmy-all }}</ref> Although Milanović dismissed these charges, SDP conspicuously put Bandić at the bottom of the [[electoral list]] for the city council, which prompted further accusations of hypocrisy from their opponents.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.tportal.hr/vijesti/lokalni-izbori-2009/18444/Milanovic-podrzava-Bnadica-s-figom-u-dzepu.html | title = Milanović podržava Bandića s figom u džepu | work = T-portal | date = April 23, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 29, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110610224452/http://www.tportal.hr/vijesti/lokalni-izbori-2009/18444/Milanovic-podrzava-Bnadica-s-figom-u-dzepu.html | archive-date = June 10, 2011 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref>

On 20 June the SDP presidency chose two of their members of parliament as candidates for the SDP nomination: [[Ivo Josipović]], a law professor and known jurist, and [[Ljubo Jurčić]], an economics professor and former Minister of the Economy, Labour and Entrepreneurship. Other candidates were allowed to be submitted, however, Milan Bandić refused.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.rtl.hr/jurcic-ili-josipovic-clanak-8819 | archive-url=https://archive.today/20120919063435/http://www.rtl.hr/jurcic-ili-josipovic-clanak-8819 | url-status=dead | archive-date=September 19, 2012 | title=Jurčić ili Josipović? | date=20 June 2009 | work=[[RTL Televizija]] | language=hr | access-date=14 December 2009 }}</ref>
On 12 July a [[Partisan primary|primary election]] was held between the two social democrats and Ivo Josipović won with 64.78% of the vote from around ten thousand party members.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.javno.com/en-croatia/ivo-josipovic-is-sdp-party-presidential-candidate_269985 | title=Ivo Josipovic is SDP Party Presidential Candidate | date=12 July 2009 | work=[[Javno.hr]] | access-date=14 December 2009 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090716215218/http://www.javno.com/en-croatia/ivo-josipovic-is-sdp-party-presidential-candidate_269985 | archive-date=16 July 2009 | url-status=dead | df=dmy-all }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | work = [[Net.hr]] | date = 2009-07-12 | url = http://arhiva.net.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/page/2009/07/12/0130006.html | title = SDP odabrao: Ivu za predsjednika! | language = hr | access-date = 2009-11-04 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20091217100439/http://arhiva.net.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/page/2009/07/12/0130006.html | archive-date = 2009-12-17 | url-status = live }}</ref>

However, the 'Bandić problem' escalated in the coming months as the media continued to speculate about his potential candidacy. Bandić started to travel throughout Croatia without a stated common agenda, and the press followed his every step and constantly asked him if he would run for the presidency. He consistently refused to comment, drawing more and more media attention with his indecisiveness.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://izbori.tportal.hr/arhiva/izbori/35338/Bandic-malo-hoce-pa-nece-u-predsjednicku-utrku.html | title=Bandić malo hoće pa neće u predsjedničku utrku | date=11 September 2009 | work=[[T-portal.hr]] | language=hr | access-date=14 December 2009 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110818185647/http://izbori.tportal.hr/arhiva/izbori/35338/Bandic-malo-hoce-pa-nece-u-predsjednicku-utrku.html | archive-date=18 August 2011 | url-status=dead | df=dmy-all }}</ref> Finally, the drama was concluded when he officially announced his candidacy for president in a speech on 5 November at 7&nbsp;am on [[Medvednica|Sljeme]] criticizing Zoran Milanović and his former party for turning against him saying that he was one of the founders of SDP emphasizing that he stood with the party through the good and the bad times.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.javno.com/hr-hrvatska/video--kao-feniks-se-dizem-i-kandidiram_280439 | title=Kao Feniks se dižem i kandidiram | date=5 November 2009 | work=[[Javno.com]] | language=hr | access-date=14 December 2009 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100107102244/http://www.javno.com/hr-hrvatska/video--kao-feniks-se-dizem-i-kandidiram_280439 | archive-date=7 January 2010 | url-status=dead | df=dmy-all }}</ref> Bandić was automatically expelled from [[Social Democratic Party of Croatia|SDP]], losing his position as a member of party's presidency and leader of the Zagreb branch of SDP.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.vecernji.hr/vijesti/bandic-izbacen-sdp-a-sljemenu-objavio-kandidaturu-clanak-45342 | title = Bandić izbačen iz SDP-a; na Sljemenu objavio kandidaturu | work = [[Večernji list]] | language = hr | date = November 4, 2009 | access-date = November 5, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20091107091113/http://www.vecernji.hr/vijesti/bandic-izbacen-sdp-a-sljemenu-objavio-kandidaturu-clanak-45342 | archive-date = November 7, 2009 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> Despite that, he declared on Croatian national television [[Croatian Radiotelevision|HRT]] that he still finds himself as true Social Democrat and that he'll proudly keep his SDP membership card nr. 2. SDP later made a statement that Bandić's membership card number was 38159.<ref>{{cite web|publisher=[[Index.hr]]|url=http://www.index.hr/vijesti/clanak/video--bandiceva-kampanja-pocela-s-neistinom-broj-iskaznice-u-sdpu-nije-2-nego-38159/458542.aspx|language=hr|title=Bandićeva kampanja počela s neistinom: Broj iskaznice u SDP-u nije "2" nego "38159"|trans-title=Bandić campaign kicks off with a fallacy: Number of the SDP membership card is not 2 but 38159|date=5 November 2009|access-date=7 November 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091108115818/http://www.index.hr/vijesti/clanak/video--bandiceva-kampanja-pocela-s-neistinom-broj-iskaznice-u-sdpu-nije-2-nego-38159/458542.aspx|archive-date=2009-11-08|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Round table presidential.jpg|thumb|300px|Round table discussion with the candidates. From left to right: Damir Kajin, Nadan Vidošević, Ivo Josipović, Andrija Hebrang, Vesna Pusić]]

By the time the nominations period started, most major political parties nominated their candidates for president:
* The governing [[Croatian Democratic Union]] nominated [[Andrija Hebrang (son)|Andrija Hebrang]], a former vice-president of the [[Croatian government]] and former Minister of Health and Social Services.
* The main opposition party [[Social Democratic Party of Croatia|SDP]] nominated [[Ivo Josipović]], a professor of law at the [[University of Zagreb]], a lecturer at the Zagreb Musical Academy and an SDP MP.
* [[Croatian People's Party – Liberal Democrats|The Croatian People's Party – Liberal Democrats]] nominated [[Vesna Pusić]], the head of the parliamentary committee in charge of overseeing the negotiation process of Croatia and the [[European Union]].
* [[Istrian Democratic Assembly|The Istrian Democratic Assembly]] nominated [[Damir Kajin]], their longtime representative in the [[Croatian Parliament]].

The major independent candidates originating in the two largest parties are:
* [[Nadan Vidošević]], the president of the Croatian Economic Chamber, formerly in HDZ
* [[Milan Bandić]], mayor of [[Zagreb]], formerly in SDP
* [[Dragan Primorac]], former Minister of Education, formerly in HDZ

Several other candidates also had a history of association with major parties:
* [[Vesna Škare Ožbolt]], former [[Ministry of Justice (Croatia)|Minister of Justice]], switched from HDZ to [[Democratic Centre (Croatia)|DC]] in 2000
* [[Miroslav Tuđman]], former head of Central Intelligence Service, formerly in HDZ in 1990s

Political party candidates were selected within party elections, or were elected by party bodies, and they are the party's sole official candidates. All parliamentary parties have a policy to support only one candidate, and if their member announces an independent nomination, he or she automatically loses their party membership. Parliamentary parties that do not candidate their member for the president, usually publicly support someone. These parties can be listed on the electoral list for the president, but sometimes their support is just a public endorsement. Regional-right wing [[Croatian Democratic Assembly of Slavonija and Baranja|HDSSB]] supported independent candidate Milan Bandić, and regional-centrist [[Alliance of Primorje-Gorski Kotar|PGS]] supported independent Nadan Vidošević.

After the election, the [[Constitution of Croatia|Constitution]] does not allow the President to be a member of any political party<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.usud.hr/default.aspx?Show=ustav_republike_hrvatske&Lang=en |title=The Constitution of the Republic of Croatia |publisher=[[Constitutional Court of the Republic of Croatia]] |access-date=2009-11-29 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080629115541/http://www.usud.hr/default.aspx?Show=ustav_republike_hrvatske&Lang=en |archive-date=2008-06-29 }}</ref> so he or she must resign from membership, if there is one.

