===First round result analysis===
[[File:2010 izbori.png|thumb|Results of the first round]]

[[File:Croatia 2009 map results.PNG|right|thumb|Results of the first round in all of Croatia's counties: the candidate with the plurality of votes in each administrative division.{{legend|#E32A30|Josipović}} {{legend|#025EB1|Hebrang}} {{legend|#9ACD32|Kajin}}]]
The first round of the election saw the lowest turnout of any presidential election in [[Croatia]]'s history with only 43.96% of all the citizens eligible to vote participating in the election compared to 50.57% five years ago and 62.98% ten years ago. Most political observers noted the public's increasing distrust in politicians and the political system as the main reason for the low turnout.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.novilist.hr/2009/12/28/gong-3A-izborni-dan-do-10-sati-b.aspx | title = Slab odaziv birača, glasovao državni vrh | work = Novi List | date = December 27, 2009 | language = hr }}{{Dead link|date=April 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

Nationwide, Ivo Josipović obtained 32% and Milan Bandić 14%, meaning the two of them combined achieved less support than Stjepan Mesić did in [[2005 Croatian presidential election|2005]] in the first round only. The fact that there were only three actual left-leaning candidates meant the left was relatively united under Josipović, while the votes of the right were spread out on several prominent candidates, including Bandić, which was one of the reasons why no right wing candidate managed to qualify for a run-off. As such, this election became historic as a run-off would feature two candidates once in the same party, Josipović, who rejoined [[Social Democratic Party of Croatia|SDP]] a year before the election, and Bandić, who left SDP a month before the election so he could run as an independent.

The candidate of the governing [[centre-right]] [[Croatian Democratic Union]] (HDZ) Andrija Hebrang came in third with 12% of the vote, a result noticeably better than most polls had predicted, but still seen by many as a debacle for HDZ as he achieved the lowest ever result for the party on a national level and also became their first presidential candidate not to secure a second round since [[Mate Granić]] in [[2000 Croatian presidential election|2000]]. Nadan Vidošević, who was seen by many as a front-runner for the presidency in the beginning of the year,<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.croportal.net/link/view/509846 | title = Istraživanje: Vidošević najpoželjniji za predsjednika, Sanader najnegativniji političar | work = Croportal.net | date = March 3, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 29, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20111002001112/http://www.croportal.net/link/view/509846 | archive-date = October 2, 2011 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> came in fourth with 11% of the vote. Dragan Primorac was the third prominent right-wing candidate who invested huge sums in his campaign,<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.vecernji.hr/vijesti/analiza-troskova-primorac-svaki-glas-platio-71-bandic-36-a-josipovic-6-kn-clanak-72840 | title = GONG-ove procjene – Analiza troškova: Primorac svaki glas platio 71, Bandić 36, a Josipović 6 kn | date = December 28, 2009 | work = [[Večernji list]] | language = hr | access-date = December 29, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20091231020617/http://www.vecernji.hr/vijesti/analiza-troskova-primorac-svaki-glas-platio-71-bandic-36-a-josipovic-6-kn-clanak-72840 | archive-date = December 31, 2009 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> but in the poll he achieved only 6%, lower than what many polls had predicted. Primorac was indeed surpassed even by the centre-left candidate Vesna Pusić who was fifth, obtaining 7.25%.

No other candidate achieved more than 5% on a national level. Left-wing Damir Kajin made a decent showing in his home county, but was nevertheless surpassed by the right-wing Miroslav Tuđman in the overall tally. Slavko Vukšić's final number of votes was even smaller than the number of signatures that originally supported his nomination.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://izbori.dnevnik.hr/clanak/vijesti/slavko-vuksic-iz-slavonije-donio-12-5-tisuca-potpisa.html | title = Slavko Vukšić iz Slavonije donio 12,5 tisuća potpisa | work = [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] | date = November 17, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 29, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20091118165529/http://izbori.dnevnik.hr/clanak/vijesti/slavko-vuksic-iz-slavonije-donio-12-5-tisuca-potpisa.html | archive-date = November 18, 2009 | url-status = dead | df = mdy-all }}</ref>

Josipović carried all 21 [[Counties of Croatia|counties]] except for two, the [[Lika–Senj County]] which was carried by Andrija Hebrang and the [[Istria County]] carried by Damir Kajin.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.izbori.hr/2009Predsjednik/indexr.html | title = Potpuni službeni rezultati izbora za predsjednika Republike Hrvatske | work = State Election Committee of the Republic of Croatia | date = December 28, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 29, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20091230113836/http://www.izbori.hr/2009Predsjednik/indexr.html | archive-date = December 30, 2009 | url-status = dead | df = mdy-all }}</ref> He also won the all major cities including the capital of Croatia, [[Zagreb]], a sort of embarrassment for Bandić who is the mayor of the city. Bandić had won the [[2009 Zagreb local elections]] with almost 150,000 votes just seven months prior, but with a turnout of 41.69%/33.62% and under the SDP banner.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.izbori.hr/2009Lokalni/rezultati2K/r_15_21_0021_000.html | title = Final result of the Mayoral election for the City of Zagreb | work = State Election Committee of the Republic of Croatia | date = June 1, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 29, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20090606064802/http://www.izbori.hr/2009Lokalni/rezultati2K/r_15_21_0021_000.html | archive-date = June 6, 2009 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> This time he won only some 59,000 votes or 15.64% in Zagreb, with a local turnout of 52.40%.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.izbori.hr/2009Predsjednik/rezultati/r_21_0000.html | title = Complete official result of the Presidential election for the City of Zagreb | work = State Election Committee of the Republic of Croatia | date = December 28, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 29, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20091230135355/http://www.izbori.hr/2009Predsjednik/rezultati/r_21_0000.html | archive-date = December 30, 2009 | url-status = dead | df = mdy-all }}</ref>

===First round election night===

As all the polls around the country closed at 7&nbsp;p.m., the first exit polls were published by the major television networks. They showed Ivo Josipović easily claiming first place, but failing to obtain an [[Two-round system|outright majority]] winning 32%. The polls showed Milan Bandić, Andrija Hebrang and Nadan Vidošević in a statistical tie with each of them achieving 14%, 12% and 11% respectively.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.najnovijevijesti.net/izlazne-ankete-josipovic-vodi-sa-327-drugi-bandic-treci-hebrang_118259 | title = Izlazne ankete: Josipović vodi sa 32,7%, drugi Bandić, treći Hebrang | work = najnovijevijesti.net | date = December 27, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 30, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110723215538/http://www.najnovijevijesti.net/izlazne-ankete-josipovic-vodi-sa-327-drugi-bandic-treci-hebrang_118259 | archive-date = July 23, 2011 | url-status = dead | df = mdy-all }}</ref> Andrija Hebrang's third place was seen as a great success as most opinion polls before the first round showed him with only single-digit approval, while Nadan Vidošević's placing was seen as a disappointment since most polls before the vote showed him battling for second place with Milan Bandić.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.javno.com/hr-hrvatska/josipovic-dobio-3244-bandic-148-posto-glasova_287745 | title = Josipović dobio 32,44, Bandić 14,8 posto glasova | work = Javno.com | date = December 28, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 30, 2009 | archive-url = https://archive.today/20130126222816/http://www.javno.com/hr-hrvatska/josipovic-dobio-3244-bandic-148-posto-glasova_287745 | archive-date = January 26, 2013 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> The polls predicted Vesna Pusić and Dragan Primorac would be the only other candidates to pass 5% with each of them collecting 7% and 5% respectively.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://izbori.dnevnik.hr/clanak/pogledajte-izlazne-ankete-nove-tv-i-ipsos-pulsa.html/ | title = Izlazne ankete: Vode Josipović i Bandić, Hebrang im za petama | work = [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] | date = December 27, 2009 | language = hr | url-status = dead | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110721100502/http://izbori.dnevnik.hr/clanak/pogledajte-izlazne-ankete-nove-tv-i-ipsos-pulsa.html/ | archive-date = July 21, 2011 }}</ref> In [[2007 Croatian parliamentary election|2007]], during the parliamentary election, all exit polls predicted SDP would narrowly win, when in the reality HDZ turned out as the victor.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://dnevnik.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/izlazne-ankete-sdp-u-blagoj-prednosti-pred-hdz-om.html | title = Izlazne ankete: SDP u blagoj prednosti pred HDZ-om | work = [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] | date = November 25, 2007 | language = hr | access-date = December 30, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110721155111/http://dnevnik.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/izlazne-ankete-sdp-u-blagoj-prednosti-pred-hdz-om.html | archive-date = July 21, 2011 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> With that in mind, Andrija Hebrang expressed conviction that when actual results were published, he would overtake Bandić and qualify for second round. He claimed that exit polls do not take into account the votes coming from the citizens living abroad and that conservative voters do not participate in exit polls as much as liberal voters.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://business.hr/hr/Naslovnica/Politika/Macek-Hebrang-sigurno-ulazi-u-drugi-krug | title = Maček: Hebrang sigurno ulazi u drugi krug | work = business.hr | date = December 27, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 30, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110721100159/http://www.business.hr/hr/Naslovnica/Politika/Macek-Hebrang-sigurno-ulazi-u-drugi-krug | archive-date = July 21, 2011 | url-status = dead | df = mdy-all }}</ref> However, the exit polls predicted the outcome almost perfectly as the country's electoral commission's results later confirmed.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.izbori.hr/2009Predsjednik/rezultati/r_00_0000.html | title = POTPUNI SLUŽBENI REZULTATI IZBORA ZA PREDSJEDNIKA REPUBLIKE HRVATSKE | work = DIP | date = December 28, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 27, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20130420222242/http://www.izbori.hr/2009Predsjednik/rezultati/r_00_0000.html | archive-date = April 20, 2013 | url-status = dead | df = mdy-all }}</ref>

Ivo Josipović gave a short speech after the exit polls were announced saying he was confident the official results would match the results they predicted. After the official results were published, he gave a victory speech shortly after midnight thanking his colleagues and especially his voters for their support and expressed conviction he would be the victor once again two weeks later. He said the citizens of Croatia have a choice between the light and the dark, with him being the light, referring to his untarnished political record, and Bandić being the dark, referring to his allegations of corruption.<ref name="josipovic-light-dark"/> Milan Bandić arrived to his campaign headquarters just a couple of minutes before midnight and just a couple of minutes after the results were announced he gave his speech. He congratulated all the other candidates and said a new race began that night. He said he would be an independent president, not a remote-control of [[Zoran Milanović]] and accused Josipović of being a project of the highest-ranking members of SDP. He claimed Croatia needs a president that works for them, not for his party.<ref name="bandic-pawn"/> While he was speaking, most television networks were showing scenes from Josipović headquarters in the corner, with Milanović observing Bandić's remarks. During the middle of his speech, right when Bandić was bashing his former party and its leader, Josipović came to the stage and all television stations switched to hear what he had to say.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/obracanje-milana-bandica.html | title = Obraćanje Milana Bandića | work = [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] | date = December 28, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 30, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110721102708/http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/obracanje-milana-bandica.html | archive-date = July 21, 2011 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref>

Andrija Hebrang accused his party's dissidents, Vidošević and Primorac, as well as most right-wing candidates for 'stealing' his votes saying the result was that two left-wingers would face-off in the second round. Despite this, he called the election a great victory of the right claiming that right-leaning candidates combined achieved better results that left-leaning candidates combined.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/obracanje-andrije-hebranga.html | title = Obraćanje Andrije Hebranga | work = [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] | date = December 28, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 30, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110721102724/http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/obracanje-andrije-hebranga.html | archive-date = July 21, 2011 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> In making this assertion, he failed to consider Bandić as left-leaning, and ignored Vidošević's appeal to the left-leaning voters as well. Vidošević conceded defeat saying that one should never blame his failures on others, but himself. He urged the public to continue believing in a better tomorrow saying that Croatia should be a country of knowledge and justice, not of corruption and divisiveness.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/nadanobracanje.html | title = Obraćanje Nadana Vidoševića | work = [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] | date = December 28, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 30, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110901133201/http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/nadanobracanje.html | archive-date = September 1, 2011 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> Pusić gave a short teary speech calling Croatia a country with a lot of potential with only the right leadership. She also noted that no government would ever be able to win without the support of [[Croatian People's Party - Liberal Democrats|HNS]].<ref>{{cite web | url = http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/obracanje-vesne-pusic.html | title = Obraćanje Vesne Pusić | work = [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] | date = December 28, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 30, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110901133634/http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/obracanje-vesne-pusic.html | archive-date = September 1, 2011 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> Primorac said he would be forming a new party which would be independent from both HDZ and SDP and said he saw a bright future for Croatia.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.rtl.hr/primorac-vracamo-se-na-velika-vrata-video-5329 | title = Primorac: Vraćamo se na velika vrata | work = [[RTL Televizija]] | date = December 28, 2009 | language = hr | access-date = December 30, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20100116143006/http://www.rtl.hr/primorac-vracamo-se-na-velika-vrata-video-5329 | archive-date = January 16, 2010 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref>

This election night also saw great drama with the war of the [[Television in Croatia|television networks]]. [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] and [[RTL Televizija]] organized the exit polls together and offered [[Croatian Radiotelevision|HRT]] to participate, however HRT refused claiming they were unaware exit polls would be permitted as electoral silence lasted until midnight. HRT showed the results of the exit polls during their prime-time news show [[Dnevnik HRT|Dnevnik]]. Nova TV and RTL Televizija objected and demanded HRT to pay for distributing their polls.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nacional.hr/clanak/74503/prisavska-posla-htv-pokrao-izlazne-ankete-novoj-tv-i-rtl-u |title=Prisavska posla: HTV 'ukrao' izlazne ankete Novoj TV i RTL-u |work=[[Nacional (weekly)|Nacional]] |date=28 December 2009 |language=hr |trans-title=Prisavlje business: Croatian Radiotelevision 'steals' Nova TV and RTL exit polls |access-date=23 July 2012 |archive-date=13 June 2011 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110613180009/http://www.nacional.hr/clanak/74503/prisavska-posla-htv-pokrao-izlazne-ankete-novoj-tv-i-rtl-u }}</ref>

===Second round result analysis===
[[File:Croatian presidential election 2010 results 2.png|thumb|Results of the second round]]

The first round of the election saw the lowest turnout for any Croatian presidential election ever, so many political pundits at the beginning of the runoff campaign predicted an even lower turnout for the second round. However, as the campaign started to become more heated and the electoral body more divided, it was evident the runoff election would have a greater turnout than the first round. The increase in turnout was 277,661 people, of which 268,166 cast valid votes. Overall, 50.13% of citizens who are eligible to vote fulfilled their public right. That's a little more than 6 percentage points higher turnout than the first round and on pair with [[2005 Croatian presidential election|five years ago]], but 10 percentage points less than [[2000 Croatian presidential election|10 years ago]] and the lowest turnout for a runoff presidential election ever.

Nationwide, Ivo Josipović achieved 1,339,385 votes, or 60.26%, while Milan Bandić won 883,222 votes, or 39.74%. Most polls before the election were showing Josipović with a hefty double-digit lead, but never as much as 20 percentage points as he achieved during the election. The difference between the two candidates came as a surprise to many pundits and analysts for two reasons: the polls usually favor the more liberal candidate since left-leaning voters tend to participate in opinion polls more than conservative voters; and the fact that most polls before the election didn't include the votes from the citizens living abroad, which was expected to be a strong boost for Bandić come Election Day. Nevertheless, the final result is similar to the result of the last several polls when only the certain voters were taken into account, which may indicate that few undecided voters actually went to the polls in the second round.

[[Image:Croatia Elections 2010.png|thumb|350px|left|'''Votes for Josipović in the 2nd round'''<br />The bottom right square represents extraterritorial voters<br />Zagreb (Z) boroughs are also shown separately]]Josipović won all [[Counties of Croatia|Croatian counties]] except for the [[Lika-Senj county]], the least populated Croatian county. Similarly, he won all cities with more than 20,000 inhabitants and all cities that are county seats, except for [[Gospić]], which is also the smallest. The capital city Zagreb was also won by Josipović, with a margin that was slightly higher than the national average. Bandić was unsuccessful in the majority of Zagreb boroughs, but he did win Lika and the extraterritorial electoral unit, where the majority of votes came from [[Bosnia and Herzegovina]]. The closest margins were in the traditional right-wing strongholds in parts of Slavonia and Dalmatia.

According to the exit polls Josipović won more than 90% of Vesna Pusić's voters and 70% of Nadan Vidošević's voters. He also managed to consolidate the traditionally [[centre-left]] voters winning 90% of [[Social Democratic Party of Croatia|his party's]] as well as more than 80% of [[Croatian People's Party - Liberal Democrats|HNS]] voters and more than 70% of the [[Centre (politics)|centrist]] [[Croatian Social Liberal Party|HSLS]] and [[centre-right]] [[Croatian Peasant Party|HSS]] voters, at the same time appealing to every fourth [[Croatian Democratic Union|HDZ]] voter. Bandić won almost 80% of all Hebrang voters as well as 57% of Primorac's voters and obtained 3 quarters of all HDZ voters, confirming the assumption that Bandić mostly appealed to the [[right (politics)|right]].<ref>{{cite web | url = http://izbori.dnevnik.hr/clanak/ivo_josipovic/hrvatska-u-crvenom-bandic-je-bio-kandidat-hdz-a.html | language = hr | title = Hrvatska 'u crvenom': Bandić je zapravo bio kandidat HDZ-a | date = January 11, 2010 | work = [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] | access-date = January 11, 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20100114021534/http://izbori.dnevnik.hr/clanak/ivo_josipovic/hrvatska-u-crvenom-bandic-je-bio-kandidat-hdz-a.html | archive-date = January 14, 2010 | url-status = dead | df = mdy-all }}</ref>

The result was not received particularly well by the Church gazette ''[[Glas Koncila]]'' editor Ivan Miklenić, who stated Josipović's legitimacy, but first pointed out that he was elected by less than a third of the total population, and claimed that he was supported by "obscure power centers". He concluded that the election is the result of a policy of continuation and "not a democratic breakthrough".<ref>{{cite news | newspaper = [[Glas Koncila]] | language = hr | url = http://www.glas-koncila.hr/rubrike_komentar.html?broj_ID=18087 | title = Potvrda kontinuiteta | author = Ivan Miklenić | date = January 17, 2010 | access-date = January 16, 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20100117073658/http://www.glas-koncila.hr/rubrike_komentar.html?broj_ID=18087 | archive-date = January 17, 2010 | url-status = dead | df = mdy-all }}</ref> The secular ''[[Jutarnji list]]'' editor Davor Butković lambasted that opinion, saying he was personally a Catholic but was offended by the notion that the Church would undermine the election result. Having a third of the electorate's votes is not relevant because not even the late [[Franjo Tuđman]] or even any single Government was ever elected with more than a half of the electorate. He concluded by asking why the Church is "insulting its faithful who voted for Josipović" and saying that as long as this kind of a stance persists, the Croatian churches will "remain empty other than on major holidays".<ref>{{cite news | newspaper = [[Jutarnji list]] | language = hr | url = http://www.jutarnji.hr/zasto-kaptol-vrijeda-svoje-vjernike-koji-su-glasali-za-josipovica/486152/ | title = Zašto Kaptol vrijeđa svoje vjernike koji su glasali za Josipovića | date = January 14, 2010 | access-date = January 16, 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20100117071930/http://www.jutarnji.hr/zasto-kaptol-vrijeda-svoje-vjernike-koji-su-glasali-za-josipovica/486152 | archive-date = January 17, 2010 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> The secular ''[[Večernji list]]'' chief commentator [[Milan Ivkošić]] once again called on Josipović to stay away from former President Mesić's "selective finger-pointing" which in his opinion particularly impacted the President's relations with [[Kaptol, Zagreb|Kaptol]], in addition to a perceived "complaisance in the defence of Croatian national interests". He also noted that the new president remains "marked" by his agnosticism in relation to the right-wing voting body, but called on the Croatians to determine a basic consensus and avoid any accusations of "treason".<ref>{{cite news | newspaper = [[Večernji list]] | language = hr | url = http://www.vecernji.hr/kolumne/drukciji-mesica-kolumna-79258 | title = Rodoljubna zanovijetanja - Drukčiji od Mesića?! | date = January 14, 2010 | access-date = January 16, 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20100117081501/http://www.vecernji.hr/kolumne/drukciji-mesica-kolumna-79258 | archive-date = January 17, 2010 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> Josipović indeed explicitly stated that he "will not step into that whole polemic" and that he has no comment.<ref>{{cite news | newspaper = [[Slobodna Dalmacija]] | language = hr | url = http://www.slobodnadalmacija.hr/Hrvatska/tabid/66/articleType/ArticleView/articleId/87572/Default.aspx | title = Josipović bez komentara na Glas Koncila, Bozanić čestitao: Promičite nacionalne interese | date = January 14, 2010 | access-date = January 16, 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20120404132236/http://www.slobodnadalmacija.hr/Hrvatska/tabid/66/articleType/ArticleView/articleId/87572/Default.aspx | archive-date = April 4, 2012 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref>

===Second round election night===

[[File:Josipovic election night.jpg|thumb|300px|Ivo Josipović prepares to give a victory speech after it was announced he would be the next president]]

When the polls around the country closed at 19:00 [[RTL Televizija|RTL]] and [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] published the results of the exit polls conducted by [[Ipsos|Ipsos PULS]]. They predicted a landslide win for Josipović, giving him an advantage of almost 30 percentage points, significantly larger than all of the second-round polls. This shifted much of the political commentary and rendered much of the expected drama moot. The Josipović headquarters started celebrating immediately after the polls closed claiming that the difference is too large for Bandić to overcome and the exit polls don't feature such a large statistical margin of error. The Bandić campaign said they'll wait for official results, but admitted it would be very hard for Bandić to overtake Josipović's 30 point lead even when the votes from abroad were tallied. The exit polls predicted the outcome well as the ratio between the two candidates was 9 to 1 for Bandić abroad, where there were no exit polls, which narrowed Josipović's victory to a final difference of 21 percentage points.

After the official results were announced at midnight it was evident that Ivo Josipović was elected the third president of the Republic. He gave a victory speech shortly after thanking everybody who voted for him, but also saying he would be the president of all, not just the left. He called his victory a step in the right direction and said he would work hard for justice and a better Croatia.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/josipovic-ovo-je-bila-pobjednicka-simfonija.html | language = hr | title = Josipović: Ovo je bila pobjednička simfonija | date = January 11, 2010 | work = [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] | access-date = January 12, 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20100117015138/http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/josipovic-ovo-je-bila-pobjednicka-simfonija.html | archive-date = January 17, 2010 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> Josipović did not finish his acceptance speech before Bandić started his concession speech, using the opportunity to decry "hate, contempt and intolerance from everyone" towards him.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.vecernji.hr/vijesti/cestitam-kolegi-josipovicu-a-vec-sutra-sam-poslu-clanak-77188 | language = hr | title = Čestitam kolegi Josipoviću, a već sutra sam na poslu | date = January 10, 2010 | work = [[Večernji list]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url = http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/bandic-priznao-poraz.html | language = hr | title = Josipović: Bandić priznao poraz | date = January 11, 2010 | work = [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] | access-date = January 12, 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110901132251/http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/bandic-priznao-poraz.html | archive-date = September 1, 2011 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url = http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/vracam-se-raditi-svoj-posao.html | language = hr | title = Vraćam se raditi svoj posao | date = January 11, 2010 | work = [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] | access-date = January 12, 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110901134252/http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/vracam-se-raditi-svoj-posao.html | archive-date = September 1, 2011 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref>
The Bandić [[self-victimization]] was ultimately not well received by both the voters and the media.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.vecernji.hr/vijesti/ipak-je-kraju-skromni-david-pobijedio-bahatog-golijata-clanak-77801 | language = hr | work = [[Večernji list]] | title = Večernjakovi kolumnisti o izboru Ive Josipovića za hrvatskog predsjednika | date = January 11, 2010 | access-date = January 12, 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20100113091702/http://www.vecernji.hr/vijesti/ipak-je-kraju-skromni-david-pobijedio-bahatog-golijata-clanak-77801 | archive-date = January 13, 2010 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref>

During election night, Milanović gave an interview to the three major television networks where he congratulated Ivo Josipović and called on [[Jadranka Kosor]] to hold a meeting with him where they would discuss the major political differences between their two parties and find a way to agree on how to help the economy further.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/milanovic-o-pobjedi-ive-josipovica.html | language = hr | title = Milanović o pobjedi Ive Josipovića | date = January 11, 2010 | work = [[Nova TV (Croatia)|Nova TV]] | access-date = January 21, 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110901133109/http://videoteka.novatv.hr/multimedia/milanovic-o-pobjedi-ive-josipovica.html | archive-date = September 1, 2011 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref> The [[Prime Minister of Croatia|prime minister]] responded the very same night when she congratulated Josipović saying she invited the opposition leader for a coffee over six months ago claiming he refused.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.net.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/page/2010/01/11/0757006.html | language = hr | work = Net.hr | title = Tko je Milanović da mene poziva na razgovor? | date = January 11, 2010 | access-date = January 21, 2010 | archive-url = https://archive.today/20120905054254/http://www.net.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/page/2010/01/11/0757006.html | archive-date = September 5, 2012 | url-status = dead | df = mdy-all }}</ref> Milanović responded the next day by saying he doesn't want finger-pointing, but a constructive discussion about jobs, the budget and tax reform. They came to an agreement the following week and they held a meeting on 21 January.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.index.hr/vijesti/clanak/kosor-i-milanovic-popili-famoznu-kavu-nismo-neprijatelji-nego-politicki-suparnici/471529.aspx | language = hr | work = Index.hr | title = Kosor i Milanović popili famoznu kavu: "Nismo neprijatelji nego politički suparnici" | date = January 21, 2010 | access-date = January 22, 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20100125035625/http://www.index.hr/vijesti/clanak/kosor-i-milanovic-popili-famoznu-kavu-nismo-neprijatelji-nego-politicki-suparnici/471529.aspx | archive-date = January 25, 2010 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref>

Although [[Croatian Radiotelevision|HRT]] admitted they crossed the line two weeks ago when they reported the results of the exit polls conducted by RTL and Nova TV, they once again 'borrowed' the results for the second round election night. This caused a stir within HRT against the main editor of the news program Hloverka Novak-Srzić who was faced with scandals long before the election night incident. Finally, exactly nine days after the election, on 19 January, she was released of her duties as chief editor.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.slobodnadalmacija.hr/Hrvatska/tabid/66/articleType/ArticleView/articleId/88247/Default.aspx | language = hr | work = [[Slobodna Dalmacija]] | title = Smijenjena Hloverka Novak Srzić: Ovo je olakšanje; Latin: Hvala Bogu! | date = January 19, 2010 | access-date = January 21, 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20120404132311/http://www.slobodnadalmacija.hr/Hrvatska/tabid/66/articleType/ArticleView/articleId/88247/Default.aspx | archive-date = April 4, 2012 | url-status = live | df = mdy-all }}</ref>

